A: I don't see anything wrong with your code. It should work perfectly fine assuming that ArrayList is indeed a valid class/type when used with Pex.

There might be two possible issues that come to my mind:


*

*You are not running Pex in your project, and it is not set up correctly.

*ArrayList class/type does not support any kind of Pex method testing. Pex only supports methods marked as [PexMethod] and can test public and non-public methods.


Try running your test case in Visual Studio with Pex enabled, see if it finds any bugs or exceptions. If Pex is set up correctly and you still have the same problem, it might be a limitation of the tool.

Another alternative could be running these tests in a different environment where Pex is correctly installed and configured. Also, you could write the tests as a normal Unit Test and see if they work fine.

If the tests still don't work even after these steps, I would suggest you to raise it as an issue in the Pex issue tracker. They might be able to help you out with it.

Also, please ensure you are running the latest version of the Pex test framework. Sometimes there might be bugs resolved in later versions.  

Another way to go about it could be to test the functionality not through the ArrayList but through the classes and methods that utilize ArrayList. If those work correctly then it would confirm that the bug is caused by the usage of the ArrayList class. 

Also, please note that the framework works best if you can target a single problem area at a time. It's recommended that ideally you have one test case per bug. Having a large number of test cases may lead to confusion when debugging issues.

If you don't have any workable solution to your problem or if you are not able to share the information you have from running Pex, then you could ask for help on online forums where Pex users hang out or on Stack Overflow under the “Pex” tag.

Finally if none of these work and you are unable to share specific details with Pex team, you may want to consider contacting their support if the bug persists. The Pex project is funded by Microsoft, usually people who file bugs report the problems they encounter and the supportive community helps to solve them. Microsoft provides a one-year free support period for users who are still using an older--------------------	    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(New_Ret == -1 ? Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1 : true);
        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the return value is negative, index of x in old array is -1 
	    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(New_Ret == -1 ? Old_arrListIndexOfX == -1 : true);
        }
    }
}

//PexTest/PexFrameworkTesting.cs
namespace PexFrameworkTesting
{
    using System;
    using System.Collections.Generic;
    using System.Linq;
    using System.Text;
    using System.Diagnostics;

    using TestFramework;
    using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;

    public class PexFrameworkTesting
    {
        [PexMethod]
        public void AddToList_Contract(List<int> listToModify, int valueToAdd)
        {
            Assume.That(listToModify != null);

            int oldCount = listToModify.Count;
            listToModify.Add(valueToAdd);
            
            Assert.That(listToModify.Count, Is.EqualTo(oldCount + 1));
            Assert.That(listToModify.Contains(valueToAdd));
        }
    }
}

